---

<DocPage
  title="useUndoable"
  eyebrow="Primitive"
  path="components/use-undoable"
  meta={meta}
  lede="Holds a mutation for a few seconds and sends it only once undo is no longer possible. Press Undo and the request is never made at all, which is the part the usual “Deleted · Undo” toast cannot do. The window survives navigation, tab close and unmount."
  description="useUndoable reference: the five-state deferred write, the held window in which nothing has been sent, flush on navigation and unmount, coalescing, key contention, the full options and result tables, accessibility and keyboard behaviour."
>
  <Section id="description" title="Description">
    <p>
      “Deleted · Undo” is normally two unrelated pieces of software. A toast library owns the button and knows
      nothing about a commit window, so its Undo is a dismiss with a callback and no timer. A data layer owns
      the mutation and cannot recall a request once it is sent. Bolt the two together and you get the version
      everybody has shipped: the <code>DELETE</code> left when the row disappeared, the toast offers Undo for as
      long as it happens to be on screen, and pressing it has to fire a second mutation that tries to put the record
      back — if your API even has one, and if nothing else touched the record in between.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>useUndoable</code> holds the write instead. The optimistic change lands locally, the request waits,
      and undo is a cancellation rather than a compensating write. Nothing to reverse, nothing to race.
    </p>

    <p>
      Underneath is a five-state machine. Every one of these is a state a user can distinguish, which is why
      <code>status</code> is an alias of <code>state</code> rather than a narrower union: collapsing any pair would
      hide the difference between <em>you can still undo this</em> and <em>this has been sent</em>.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>idle</code></strong> — nothing is deferred. Before the first <code>schedule()</code>,
        after an undo, after <code>reset()</code>, and one second after a commit landed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>held</code></strong> — the window is open and
        <strong>nothing has been sent</strong>. The user sees the row gone and an Undo control; the server has
        heard nothing at all. This is the only state in which <code>canUndo</code> is true. The window timer and
        the leave subscription are effects of this state, so they exist exactly while there is a write to lose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>committing</code></strong> — the request is in flight. <code>canUndo</code> is false and
        <code>aria-busy</code> is true. Undo is gone, which is the truthful thing to render: there is nothing left
        to cancel. An Undo that still “works” here is the bug the machine exists to prevent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>committed</code></strong> — the write landed. The entry stays observable for one second so
        a view can swap “Deleted · Undo” for “Deleted” rather than having the message vanish mid-sentence, then
        returns to <code>idle</code>. That second is a constant, not a prop, because it is not a user
        decision.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>failed</code></strong> — the commit itself rejected. The batch is kept so a retry sends the
        same items, the failure is normalised, and the message is announced assertively, because the interface has
        already claimed the change was made and a user who has moved on still needs to hear that it was not.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The one distinction worth learning</h3>

    <p>
      <code>held</code> and <code>committing</code> are the load-bearing pair. Undo is possible in exactly one of
      them, because in the other the request has already left. Every ecosystem Undo button that dismisses a toast
      after the <code>DELETE</code> has landed is that distinction missing.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the honest UI is not “show Undo for six seconds”. It is “render the control while
      <code>canUndo</code>, and stop rendering it the moment that goes false”.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="demo" title="Live demo">
    <p>
      A real call into the library. The commit is a real promise that increments
      <code>requests sent</code> when it runs, so undo preventing the write is something you can watch rather than
      something the page claims.
    </p>

    <Demo
      title="Delete, with the write held back"
      hint="5000 ms window · 400 ms commit"
      note="Delete a row: it goes immediately and requests sent does not move. Press Undo and it never moves. Let the countdown run out and the write goes once, with every row you deleted in it."
    >
      <UndoableDemo client:visible />
    </Demo>
  </Section>

  <Section id="basic-usage" title="Basic usage">
    <Snippet code={basicCode} name="src/invoices/InvoiceList.tsx" />

    <p>
      Three things about that call site. The delete handler touches local state only — there is no request in
      it, which is the whole point. <code>commit</code> is the only place the server is called, and it receives
      the batch. <code>rollback</code> runs on undo and only on undo, so it can be the plain inverse of the optimistic
      step rather than a second API call.
    </p>

    <p>
      The undo control is rendered on <code>canUndo</code> and spread from
      <code>undoProps</code>. That spread is what carries the accessible name, and it is the reason
      <code>altText</code> is required rather than optional.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="note" title="This renders nothing">
      <p>
        There is no toast, no progress ring and no CSS in the library. <code>useUndoable</code> hands you a state,
        a count, a live countdown and a set of props; the markup is yours. See
        <a href="#toast">pairing with a toast</a> below for the one wiring detail that matters.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="survives" title="What it survives">
    <p>
      A deferred write has four ways of dying that a hand-rolled version normally does not cover. Each of
      these is behaviour in the machine, not advice.
    </p>

    <h3>Navigation</h3>

    <p>
      The failure: the user deletes a row, clicks through to another page inside the window, and the write is
      never sent. The row comes back on the next load and nothing told them. This is the most common way the
      pattern loses data, and it is why <code>flushOn</code> defaults to both exits rather than to none.
    </p>

    <p>
      While the state is <code>held</code>, the machine subscribes to the navigation port and commits on the
      way out. The subscription is an effect of that state, so it is torn down the moment the window closes —
      a listener that outlived the window would commit a batch that had already been undone.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={flushOnCode} name="flush-on.tsx" />

    <h3>Why pagehide and visibilitychange, not beforeunload</h3>

    <p>
      The DOM port listens for <code>pagehide</code> on the window and for
      <code>visibilitychange</code> on the document, and raises both as the
      <code>pagehide</code> reason. <code>beforeunload</code> is used for something else entirely: blocking an exit
      with the browser's own prompt.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>beforeunload</code> is skipped outright when a tab is discarded under memory pressure and when the
        page enters the back-forward cache, so a window flushed only from there is lost in exactly the cases where
        the user did not choose to leave.
      </li>
      <li>
        Registering a <code>beforeunload</code> listener can itself make a page ineligible for the back-forward
        cache, which is a performance cost paid on every page view to catch an exit that <code>pagehide</code>
        already reports.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>unload</code> is worse: unreliable on mobile and deprecated.
      </li>
      <li>
        A backgrounded mobile tab may never run script again. A
        <code>visibilitychange</code> to hidden is the only warning some devices give, so it is treated as the page
        going away rather than waited out.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The port also reports whether it can actually fire, through <code>flushable()</code>. Subscribing proves
      nothing: core's own fallback navigation port returns a working disposer and never calls the handler, so
      a subscription is not evidence that a flush will happen. When a window opens, <code>flushOn</code> is not
      empty and the port reports that it cannot flush, a development build reports{" "}
      <a href={href("diagnostics/ux1007")}>UX1007</a>. The React adapter's port reports true, so with the DOM
      ports in place the rule stays quiet.
    </p>

    <h3>Unmount</h3>

    <p>
      The failure: the user deletes a row and the list unmounts inside the window — a modal closes, a tab
      panel swaps, a route transition tears the subtree down without the navigation port hearing about it. The
      service stops, the timer goes with it, and the write disappears.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the hook flushes on the way out. Its cleanup is synchronous rather than deferred, because the service
      is stopped from a microtask and a flush queued behind that would be sent to a dead machine and silently
      dropped. The flush is guarded on <code>canUndo</code>, which is also why React's development
      double-mount is harmless: the window cannot already be open at that point.
    </p>

    <p>
      The matching decision sits in the machine: disposing an in-flight commit does
      <em>not</em> abort it. Everywhere else in the library teardown aborts the request. Here the request is left
      to land, because the user was told the change had happened and aborting would discard a delete they are owed.
    </p>

    <h3>Coalescing</h3>

    <p>
      The failure: five deletes in a row produce five stacked toasts, each with its own timer, and five
      separate requests. The fourth toast pushes the first one off screen before its window has closed, and
      the user has no idea which Undo belongs to what.
    </p>

    <p>
      Schedules sharing a <code>coalesce</code> tag append to the open entry and restart its window, as one entry
      with a count. It is a self-transition, so the entry keeps its identity and the open subscriptions are not
      torn down and rebuilt once per keypress during a burst. The commit is then a single call holding every item,
      which is also what your API probably wanted.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={coalesceCode} name="coalesce.tsx" />

    <p>
      Without a tag — or with a schedule whose tag does not match — a second entry would be a second write to
      the same resource overlapping the first. The machine flushes what is held and queues the newcomer behind
      it; the queued items open their own window as soon as that flush settles. Two writes to one resource
      never overlap, and nothing is dropped.
    </p>

    <h3>Key contention</h3>

    <p>
      The failure: the row is deleted, the window is open, and a save on the same record lands in the middle
      of it. Now the order of two writes to one record is decided by the network.
    </p>

    <p>
      Declare a <code>key</code> and it is held from the moment the window opens until the commit settles or the
      entry is undone — across the window, not just across the request. Every hook that declares the same key contends
      through one registry, so{" "}
      <a href={href("components/use-action")}>useAction</a> and <code>useUndoable</code> serialise against each
      other without knowing one another exists.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={keyCode} name="src/invoices/row.tsx" />

    <p>
      If another holder already has the key, the window still opens. Refusing to schedule would drop a change
      the user has already been shown as done, which is worse than two holders of one key; the second entry
      simply never held it, so settling cannot release someone else's.
    </p>

    <p>There are 78 tests in the repository and none of them sleep.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="api" title="API">
    <p>
      <code>useUndoable</code> takes one options object. <code>commit</code> and <code>altText</code> are required;
      everything else has a default, and <code>announce</code>, <code>classify</code> and
      <code>messages</code> fall back to <code>UnruffledProvider</code> when one is mounted.
    </p>

    <PropsTable rows={optionRows} caption="useUndoable options" />

    <h3>What it returns</h3>

    <p>
      Everything from <code>UndoableApi</code>, plus the four values the React layer adds:
      <code>undoProps</code>, <code>getUndoProps</code>, <code>remainingMs</code> and
      <code>progress</code>.
    </p>

    <PropsTable rows={resultRows} nameHeading="Value" caption="useUndoable return value" />

    <h3>A commit that failed</h3>

    <p>
      <code>failed</code> keeps the batch, so retrying is the same write rather than a new one.
      <code>flush()</code> is the retry; <code>reset()</code> is the dismissal. There is no automatic retry here:
      the user watched this change happen several seconds ago, so the decision to try again belongs to them.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={failedCode} name="commit-failed.tsx" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="accessibility" title="Accessibility">
    <h3>altText is required by the type</h3>

    <p>
      <code>altText</code> is not optional on <code>UndoableProps</code>, so a call site without it does not
      compile. It becomes <code>aria-label</code> on <code>undoProps</code>, which means the accessible name
      arrives with the spread rather than being something you have to remember. An icon-only Undo with no name
      is the most common way this pattern ships broken: the window is correct, the request is correctly
      deferred, and the one control that cancels it is unreachable by keyboard and screen reader.
    </p>

    <h3>The undo control is never disabled</h3>

    <p>
      <code>UndoProps</code> has no <code>disabled</code> key, and
      <code>UndoPropOverrides</code> types it as <code>never</code>, so
      <code>getUndoProps</code> rejects one. A disabled element leaves the tab order and throws the user's focus
      to the top of the document, which is a strange thing to do to someone who is mid-decision about a deletion.
      Once the window has closed there is nothing to cancel, so stop rendering the control: read
      <code>canUndo</code> and unmount it rather than leaving a dead button behind.
    </p>

    <h3>What is announced</h3>

    <p>
      Three of the four announcements are opt-in, which is the opposite of
      <a href={href("components/use-action")}>useAction</a>. Supply
      <code>announce.scheduled</code> and it is read politely when the window opens, which is the only moment undo
      is reachable and therefore the only moment a screen-reader user can act on it — an open window nobody announced
      is a delete with no undo for anyone not watching the corner of the screen.
      <code>announce.undone</code> and <code>announce.committed</code> are read politely too. Omit any of them and
      it is silent.
    </p>

    <p>
      The fourth needs no configuration: a failed commit announces the normalised failure message assertively.
      Assertive on purpose — the interface has already claimed the change was made.
      <code>announce: false</code> turns off all four. None of this reaches anyone unless
      <code>Announcer</code> is mounted once near the root; see{" "}
      <a href={href("components/announcer")}>Announcer</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Put <code>attrs</code> on the element that hosts the control. It carries
      <code>aria-busy</code> while the commit is in flight, so assistive technology knows the region is updating,
      and <code>data-count</code> so coalesced copy can read “3 rows deleted” from CSS or from the DOM.
    </p>

    <h3>The countdown under reduced motion</h3>

    <p>
      <code>remainingMs</code> buckets to 100 ms normally and to 1000 ms under
      <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>. The number stays accurate and keeps counting: a user
      deciding whether to press Undo needs the time more than the animation, so reduction means less motion,
      not less information. Freezing or hiding the countdown would withhold the one fact that makes the
      control usable.
    </p>

    <p>
      Hand <code>progress</code> to CSS as a custom property rather than driving a ring from React state, and hide
      the ring under reduction while leaving the number visible.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={progressCode} lang="css" name="src/styles/undo.css" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="keyboard" title="Keyboard">
    <Keys rows={keyRows} />

    <p>
      The hook synthesises no keyboard activation, because <code>undoProps</code> sets
      <code>type: "button"</code> and a native button already turns Enter and Space into a click. If your design
      system insists on a non-button element, give it <code>role="button"</code> and a tabindex of 0 and wire the
      keys yourself — or use a button, which brings activation, the correct role and platform shortcuts you will
      otherwise reimplement one at a time.
    </p>

    <p>
      One thing worth checking by hand: that the undo control is reachable by Tab from wherever focus actually
      is when the window opens. A toast portalled to the end of the document is often several Tab stops away
      from the row the user just deleted, and a six-second window is not long enough to go looking.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="toast" title="Pairing with a toast">
    <p>
      This library renders nothing, so the toast is yours. That is the honest trade: it composes over whatever
      notification system you already have instead of competing with it, and in exchange you wire two things
      together.
    </p>

    <p>
      The wiring detail that matters is the timer. Your toast library has one; so does the machine. If you let
      both run, they will disagree — and they disagree in the direction that loses data, because a toast that
      auto-dismisses at its own 5000 ms takes the Undo control away while the window is still open, and a
      toast that outlives the window leaves an Undo on screen after the <code>DELETE</code> has gone out. Coalescing
      makes it worse: a merged repeat restarts the machine's window and your toast knows nothing about it.
    </p>

    <p>
      So derive the toast's lifetime from <code>remainingMs</code>, render it on
      <code>canUndo</code>, and give it a stable key so a merge updates the existing toast rather than
      stacking a new one.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={toastCode} name="src/toast/undo-toast.tsx" />

    <Callout tone="warning" title="Dismissing the toast is not undoing">
      <p>
        If the user closes the toast by hand, close the toast. Do not call <code>undo()</code>, and do not
        call
        <code>flush()</code> either unless that is genuinely what your product means — one of those discards a write
        the user asked for and the other sends it early. Letting the window run out is almost always the right answer,
        and the write still goes if they navigate away first.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="mistakes" title="Common mistakes">
    <Mistake
      title="An Undo button that never gets the accessible name"
      why="altText is required, so this compiles and the name still never reaches the DOM: handing undo straight to your own onClick skips the spread that carries aria-label. The window is correct, the request is correctly deferred, and the one control that cancels it is an unlabelled icon. A screen-reader user hears “button”, and a six-second window is not long enough to go exploring."
      bad={`// altText is set, and nothing uses it.
<button onClick={deletion.undo}>
  <UndoIcon aria-hidden="true" />
</button>`}
      good={`// The spread carries type="button" and aria-label from altText.
<button {...deletion.undoProps}>
  <UndoIcon aria-hidden="true" />
</button>

// Per-control label, when one Undo is not like the others:
<button {...deletion.getUndoProps({ altText: "Undo removing 3 invoices" })}>
  <UndoIcon aria-hidden="true" />
</button>`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Running your own setTimeout beside the window"
      why="Now there are two timers for one decision, and nothing keeps them in step. The machine's window restarts when a repeat coalesces and closes early when the user navigates; your timeout does neither. Whichever fires first wins, so the Undo control either disappears while the write is still cancellable or stays on screen after the DELETE has gone out. The window is already a state — read it."
      bad={`const [showUndo, setShowUndo] = useState(false);

const remove = (row: Row) => {
  setRows((current) => current.filter((item) => item.id !== row.id));
  deletion.schedule(row);
  setShowUndo(true);
  setTimeout(() => setShowUndo(false), 6000);
};`}
      good={`const remove = (row: Row) => {
  setRows((current) => current.filter((item) => item.id !== row.id));
  deletion.schedule(row);
};

// One timer, and it is the one that decides whether undo is possible.
{deletion.canUndo ? (
  <UndoToast
    remainingMs={deletion.remainingMs}
    count={deletion.count}
    undoProps={deletion.undoProps}
  />
) : null}`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Calling the server in the optimistic step"
      why="The write then happens twice: once from the handler and once from commit when the window closes. Worse, the first one has already landed, so undo cannot prevent anything — the user presses it, rollback puts the row back locally, and the record is gone on the server. This is the original bug reintroduced inside the primitive that exists to fix it. The optimistic step is local state only."
      bad={`const remove = (row: Row) => {
  setRows((current) => current.filter((item) => item.id !== row.id));
  api.deleteRow(row.id); // already gone
  deletion.schedule(row);
};`}
      good={`const deletion = useUndoable<Row>({
  // The only place the server is called.
  commit: (batch) => api.deleteRows(batch.map((row) => row.id)),
  rollback: (batch) => setRows((current) => [...current, ...batch]),
  altText: "Undo delete",
});

const remove = (row: Row) => {
  setRows((current) => current.filter((item) => item.id !== row.id));
  deletion.schedule(row);
};`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Treating undo as a second mutation that reverses the first"
      why="A compensating write is not an undo. It needs an endpoint that may not exist, it fails on its own account, it restores a record with a new id or a new updated_at, and it loses whatever the deletion cascaded. It also cannot be honest about ordering: between the DELETE and the restore, other clients saw the record gone. In held nothing has been sent, so undo is a cancellation — rollback puts your local state back and the request is never made."
      bad={`const deletion = useUndoable<Row>({
  commit: (batch) => api.deleteRows(batch.map((row) => row.id)),
  // Called on undo, when nothing has been deleted yet.
  rollback: (batch) => api.restoreRows(batch.map((row) => row.id)),
  altText: "Undo delete",
});`}
      good={`const deletion = useUndoable<Row>({
  commit: (batch) => api.deleteRows(batch.map((row) => row.id)),
  // The inverse of the optimistic step, and nothing more.
  rollback: (batch) => setRows((current) => [...current, ...batch]),
  altText: "Undo delete",
});`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Disabling the undo control as the window runs out"
      why="A disabled element leaves the tab order, so the user who was reaching for Undo has their focus thrown to the top of the document at the exact moment the control stopped working. There is also nothing to disable: once canUndo is false the machine ignores undo() outright. Stop rendering the control instead. UndoProps has no disabled key and UndoPropOverrides types it as never, so the only way to get one is to write it on your own element."
      bad={`<button {...deletion.undoProps} disabled={!deletion.canUndo}>
  Undo
</button>`}
      good={`{deletion.canUndo ? <button {...deletion.undoProps}>Undo</button> : null}`}
    />

    <Mistake
      title="Emptying flushOn to silence UX1007"
      why="The rule is reporting that the navigation port cannot fire, which means the window dies on navigation and tab close. An empty flushOn is the opt-out for a deliberate decision that the write may be lost, so using it to quiet the console converts a warning into silent data loss. Fix the port instead: the React adapter supplies a working one, and the rule is quiet by default because of it. Reach for the opt-out only when losing the write is genuinely acceptable, and say so in the code."
      bad={`// Console is quiet. The DELETE now dies on route change.
const deletion = useUndoable<Row>({ commit, altText: "Undo", flushOn: [] });`}
      good={`// Nothing to configure: createDomPorts reports flushable() === true.
const deletion = useUndoable<Row>({ commit, altText: "Undo" });

// Or, deliberately, with the reason written down:
// Draft reordering is re-derived on load, so a lost flush costs nothing.
const reorder = useUndoable<Row>({ commit, altText: "Undo", flushOn: [] });`}
    />
  </Section>
</DocPage>
